{"reference":"John 4","book":"John","chapter":4,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"Journey and well scene","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter’s movement from Judea through Samaria and on to Galilee supplies its concrete journey frame. Within that journey, Sychar is described as near the land associated with Jacob and Joseph, and Jacob’s well becomes the setting for Jesus’ conversation with the Samaritan woman.","verse_refs":["John 4:3-4","John 4:43","John 4:5-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_john_004_1653d61d06c91a22e3f8e5b9","reader_path_john_004_3d780840fff76700275d113c"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the conversation carefully","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Several cautions help with this conversation. Jesus’ statement that “salvation is from the Jews” places him and salvation’s story within the Jewish people and their Scriptures; it should not be read as erasing Jewish identity or treating Samaritans as inferior. The Gospel’s term often translated “the Jews” can refer differently according to context—to Jewish people, Judeans, festival crowds, or particular authorities—so it should not automatically be treated as a single hostile ethnic group. The woman is also an extended theological conversation partner and later a witness to her city. Although Jesus speaks of her previous husbands, the passage does not say that she was guilty of adultery or prostitution, so those labels go beyond what the text states.","verse_refs":["John 4:19-26","John 4:4-42","John 4:9-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_john_004_6e99fe5db3da0a811617abe7"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}